        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(polls): add default maximum poll option count",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- Problem: Matrix polls had no outbound `pollMaxOptions` limit while the Matrix protocol (MSC3381) caps poll options at 20. Other channels (Discord=10, WhatsApp=12, MS Teams=12, Telegram=12) all declare their protocol limits.\n- Solution: Declare `pollMaxOptions: 20` in Matrix-owned outbound code and pass `maxOptions: 20` explicitly in `buildPollStartContent`. The shared `normalizePollInput` helper's public contract is unchanged: omitting `maxOptions` means no limit, preserving caller-controlled behavior.\n- What changed:\n  - `extensions/matrix/src/channel.ts` — added `pollMaxOptions: 20` to `matrixChannelOutbound`\n  - `extensions/matrix/src/matrix/poll-types.ts` — `buildPollStartContent` passes `maxOptions: 20` explicitly to `normalizePollInput`\n  - `src/polls.test.ts` — locks in omitted-`maxOptions` = no limit contract\n  - `extensions/matrix/src/matrix/poll-types.test.ts` — 20-option boundary tests (accept 20, reject 21)\n  - `extensions/matrix/src/channel.message-adapter.test.ts` — asserts `pollMaxOptions` on static outbound adapter\n- What did NOT change: `normalizePollInput` omitted-limit semantics; `src/polls.ts`; other channel implementations; no unrelated UI/avatar changes.\n\n## ClawSweeper review fixes (all P1 findings addressed)\n\n- **P1: Keep the generic poll limit caller-configured** — `src/polls.ts` is untouched. `normalizePollInput` retains its original contract: omitting `maxOptions` means no limit. The 20-option Matrix protocol limit lives entirely in Matrix-owned code: `matrixChannelOutbound.pollMaxOptions = 20` and `buildPollStartContent(..., { maxOptions: 20 })`. A regression test locks in the omitted-limit = no limit behavior.\n- **P1: Drop the unrelated avatar commit** — The branch has been force-pushed from a clean `origin/main` base. All unrelated Control UI avatar changes from #104912 are removed.\n- **P1: Rebase to clean mergeable head** — The branch is now a single commit on top of latest `origin/main`, no conflicts.\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\n- **Behavior or issue addressed:** Matrix polls were missing the 20-option protocol limit. Now enforced at the Matrix boundary.\n- **Real environment tested:** Vitest unit tests for Matrix poll types, Matrix outbound adapter, and shared poll helper contract.\n- **Exact steps or command run after this patch:**\n  ```bash\n  pnpm test src/polls.test.ts\n  pnpm test extensions/matrix/src/matrix/poll-types.test.ts\n  node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/matrix/src/channel.message-adapter.test.ts\n  ```\n- **Evidence after fix:**\n  ```text\n  # src/polls.test.ts — 7 passed\n  - \"does not limit options when maxOptions is omitted\" → 50 options accepted ✓\n\n  # extensions/matrix/src/matrix/poll-types.test.ts — 11 passed\n  - \"accepts exactly 20 poll options per MSC3381\" → answers.length = 20 ✓\n  - \"rejects more than 20 poll options per MSC3381\" → throws \"at most 20\" ✓\n\n  # extensions/matrix/src/channel.message-adapter.test.ts — 6 passed\n  - \"declares a 20-option poll maximum...\" → outbound.pollMaxOptions = 20 ✓\n  ```\n- **Observed result after fix:** Matrix 20-option protocol limit enforced at Matrix boundary. Public helper contract preserved (omitted `maxOptions` = no limit).\n\n## Regression Test Plan\n\n- Coverage level: Unit test\n- Target test files: `src/polls.test.ts`, `extensions/matrix/src/matrix/poll-types.test.ts`, `extensions/matrix/src/channel.message-adapter.test.ts`\n- Scenarios locked in:\n  - Matrix: 20 options accepted, 21 rejected (protocol boundary in `buildPollStartContent`)\n  - Matrix outbound: `pollMaxOptions = 20` declared on static adapter\n  - Public helper: omitted `maxOptions` does not limit (contract preserved)\n\n## Root Cause\n\n- Root cause: Matrix outbound adapter did not declare `pollMaxOptions`, unlike all other poll channel adapters (Discord, WhatsApp, MS Teams, Telegram).\n- Missing detection / guardrail: No Matrix-owned poll option limit enforcement.\n\n---\n\n@clawsweeper re-review",
